               <msContents>
                  <summary>A legal notebook (possibly once part of a larger manuscript) containing <title type="desc" key="work_14739">statutes</title>, writs, and related material, the articles of the eyre, Wiltshire pleas, and related notes.</summary>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Top_Wilts_e_3-item1" >
                     <locus>(fols. 1r-6v)</locus>
                     <title>Articles of the Eyre</title>
                     <rubric>Capitula Corone de Itinere Iusticiariorum</rubric>
                     <incipit>De veteribus placitis corone qui alias fuerunt coram iusticiar'</incipit>
                     <explicit><locus>(fol. 3r)</locus> &amp; iusticia suffocantur</explicit>
                     <rubric>Noua capitula</rubric>
                     <incipit>Quot &amp; quo maneria</incipit>
                     <explicit>conquesti fuerint</explicit>
                     <note>The last articles are 'De hiis qui ceperunt boues uel equos ...' and 'De hiis qui vindictam fecerunt', articles 5 and 6 from the 1280 version of the articles in Cam's enumeration: Helen M. Cam, <title>Studies in the hundred rolls : some aspects of thirteenth-century administration</title> (Oxford, 1921), pp. 98-99.</note>
                     <listBibl><bibl><title>Statutes of the Realm</title>, I, 234-238</bibl></listBibl>
                     <note>At the head of fol. 1r is '.v.'</note>
                  <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus>(fols. 7r-8r)</locus>
                     <note>Wiltshire verdicts, temp. Edward I, relating to the first three articles of the Noua capitula ('quot et que maneria rex habet in manu sua', 'que etiam maneria esse solent', 'de feodis')</note>
                     
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang></msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <note>Fols. 8v-9r contain items added in the 14th century:</note>
                     <msItem>
                     <locus>(fol. 8v)</locus>
                     <rubric>Ugeford sancti Jacobi pro abbatissa Wilton'</rubric>
                        <note>Notes on the descent of three tenements in <placeName key="place_104">Ugford St James</placeName> in relation to <orgName key="org_150348138">Wilton Abbey</orgName>, followed by a memorandum 'de ten' alien' in Wilton' per priorem de Brommor'' [Breamore, Hampshire].</note> <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus>(fol. 8v)</locus>
                     <rubric>Breve de indictamento habendo</rubric>
                     <note>From J. de R. sheriff of Wiltshire, reciting a writ of <foreign>certiorari super causa captionis</foreign> from King Edward, concerning William de Bakestok and Alice his wife in the gaol of <placeName key="place_7011225">Wilton</placeName>.</note> <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus>(fol. 9r)</locus>
                     <rubric>Warent' pro burgens' Wilton'</rubric>
                     <note>Undated letter from the mayor (Robert Gilberd) and burgesses of <placeName key="place_7011225">Wilton</placeName> testifying that Robert Sirman is a burgess and member of the gild merchant.</note>
                     <note>Followed by an undated obligation of T. de B. to A. de C.</note> <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem></msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus>(fol. 9v)</locus>
                     <rubric>Incipit modum faciendi homagium &amp; fidelitatem</rubric>
                     <listBibl><bibl>Form of homage and fealty: <title>Statutes of the Realm</title>, I, 227-8</bibl></listBibl>
                  <textLang mainLang="xno">Anglo-Norman</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  
                  <msItem><locus>(fols. 9v-10r)</locus><rubric>Incipiunt dies communes in banco</rubric> 
                     <listBibl><bibl> <title>Statutes of the Realm</title>, I, 208</bibl></listBibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem><locus>(fol. 10r-v)</locus><rubric>Incipiunt articuli contra prohibicionem regis</rubric> 
                     <listBibl><bibl><title>Statutes of the Realm</title>, I, 227 (Circumspecte agatis, 13 Edw. I, 1285)</bibl></listBibl><textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem><locus>(fol. 10v)</locus><rubric>Breve de odio et atya</rubric>
                     <listBibl><bibl><title>Early Registers of Writs</title>, ed. E. da Haas and G. Hall (Selden Society, 1970), p. 66 (de odio et atia, with the clause 'nisi indictatus')</bibl></listBibl>
                     <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
                  </msItem>
